<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thucydides</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 460–c. 400 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thucydides was an Athenian general and historian, author of the History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. Exiled after failing to save Amphipolis in 424 BCE, he wrote a contemporary account of the war down to 411 BCE, based on his own observation and inquiry. Its analysis of power, speeches and political motive made it a founding work of political history.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thucydides.jpg" alt="Thucydides" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: shakko · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thucydides_pushkin01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Herodotus is said to have given readings of his work in Athens, and <b>Thucydides</b> wrote the history of its war with Sparta.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Resistance was strong where Thebes was feared: Plataea, according to <b>Thucydides</b>, placed itself under Athenian protection, traditionally in 519 BCE, and in 506 BCE the Boeotians and Chalcidians were defeated by Athens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Theopompus, himself a Chian, claimed that the Chians were the first Greeks after the Spartans and Thessalians to use slaves, and the first to buy them from abroad rather than subjugate a native population, and <b>Thucydides</b> wrote that it had more slaves than any Greek state except Sparta.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus and <b>Thucydides</b> record the legend that the Argead kings of Macedon descended from Temenus, king of Argos, and so from Heracles and Zeus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thucydides</b> noted that the Odrysians, unlike the Persians, made it a custom to receive gifts rather than give them, so that nothing could be done without one.</blockquote><a href="/empire/odrysian-kingdom">Odrysian Kingdom · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Thucydides</b> records that the Corinthian shipwright Ameinocles built four ships for the Samians around 700 BCE, and in the Lelantine War the island is said to have sided with Chalcis while its rival Miletus supported Eretria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Halimous, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.91,23.73&amp;year=-421" aria-label="Halimous on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5142 801 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Thucydides"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5592 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5592 1071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.91,23.73&amp;year=-421">What was here in 421 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
